# 4
h1(d, "4  Stage 1: data acquisition and harmonisation")
h2(d, "4.1  Inputs")
table(d, pd.DataFrame([["ember_generation_yearly.csv", "Ember", "104,273 rows × 24 cols; 1985–2025; long format with one row per area, year and electricity source", "CC BY 4.0"],
                       ["owid-energy-data.csv", "Our World in Data", "23,377 rows × 130 cols; 1900–2025; wide format, one row per country-year", "CC BY 4.0"]], columns=["File", "Provider", "Shape", "Licence"]), caption="Table 3. Raw datasets", fs=8.5, widths=[4.2, 2.6, 7.0, 2.2], align_right_from=9)
h2(d, "4.2  Steps")
bullets(d, ["Filter Ember to Area type = 'Country or economy' and ten countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania (Ember name 'Tanzania (the United Republic of)'), Ethiopia, Rwanda, Burundi, Zambia, Mozambique, Congo (DRC) and Sudan. South Sudan and Somalia were excluded because of short or sparse series.",
            "Pivot generation to one row per country-year with sources as columns; fill missing coal, gas and nuclear with zero (they are structural zeros, not missing data).",
            "Rename 'Other renewables' to geothermal_other_ren, and 'Total generation' to generation; pull power-sector emissions and intensity from the 'Total generation' rows.",
            "Merge population, GDP and energy per capita from OWID by country-year (DR Congo name aligned).",
            "Derive demand_per_capita_kwh = demand × 10^9 / population and gdp_per_capita = gdp / population.",
            "Write regional_panel.csv (252 rows × 26 columns) and kenya_annual.csv (26 rows)."])
h2(d, "4.3  Checks")
para(d, "Three accounting identities and one cross-source comparison were evaluated for Kenya for all years; results are in the Final Report, Table 2. All identities held to within 0.01%, and Ember and OWID matched within 0.06%, which is expected because OWID derives its electricity series from Ember.")
h2(d, "4.4  Data issues identified")
bullets(d, ["Kenya's geothermal capacity is reported as zero in Ember although generation is 6.4 TWh (2025). Capacity-based reserve margins were therefore not computed; capacity is used only for thermal plant.", "OWID GDP for Kenya ends in 2022.", "Direct download from GitHub raw, the World Bank API and the Ember website was blocked by the workspace's network policy; the author supplied the files manually. No data were simulated."])

# 5
h1(d, "5  Stage 2: demand forecasting")
h2(d, "5.1  Target and transformation")
para(d, "The target is electricity demand D_t (TWh), 2000–2025, modelled as y_t = ln D_t, so that all models describe proportional growth and forecasts are positive by construction.")
h2(d, "5.2  Models")
table(d, pd.DataFrame([
  ["Drift (RW+drift)", "ŷ_{T+h} = y_T + h · mean(Δy)", "None"],
  ["Recent drift", "ŷ_{T+h} = y_T + h · mean of last 8 Δy", "Window w = 8"],
  ["Log-linear trend", "ŷ_{T+h} = a + b(T+h), OLS on t = 0..T", "a, b"],
  ["ARIMA(1,1,0)+drift", "Δy_t = c + φ Δy_{t-1} + ε_t, estimated by conditional least squares; φ clipped to ±0.95; forecasts iterate the recursion", "c, φ"],
  ["ETS(A,Ad,N) on logs", "ℓ_t = ℓ_{t-1}+φb_{t-1}+αe_t; b_t = φb_{t-1}+αβe_t; ŷ_{T+h} = ℓ_T + (φ+…+φ^h) b_T", "α∈[.01,.99], β∈[.01,.99], φ∈[.80,.98] by SSE minimisation"],
  ["GDP-driver (conditional)", "Δy_t = a + b Δln GDP_t; forecast uses realised GDP in backtest", "a, b"],
  ["Ensemble (mean)", "Mean of log-forecasts of Drift, ARIMA and Log-linear trend", "Equal weights"]],
  columns=["Model", "Specification", "Parameters"]), caption="Table 4. Forecasting models (all on log demand)", fs=8.5, widths=[3.6, 9.4, 3.4], align_right_from=9)
h2(d, "5.3  Backtest design")
para(d, f"Rolling-origin evaluation: for each origin year T from 2010 to 2024 (minimum training window 11 observations, expanding thereafter), each model was refitted using data up to T only and forecasts for h = 1, 2, 3 were recorded against the realised value. This yields {len(bt[bt.model=='Drift (RW+drift)'])} out-of-sample forecasts per univariate model (15, 14 and 13 at horizons 1, 2 and 3) and {len(bt[bt.model=='GDP-driver (conditional)'])} for the GDP-driver model, whose targets are limited to years with GDP (≤ 2022). Metrics: MAPE, RMSE (TWh), mean percentage bias, sd of log errors and MAE relative to the drift model.")
h2(d, "5.4  Prediction intervals")
para(d, f"Let s_1 be the RMS of the ensemble's one-step log errors from the backtest ({ff['sigma_h']['1']:.4f}). The h-step log-forecast standard deviation is assumed to be s_1·√h, and intervals are exp(ln F_h ± z·s_1√h) with z = 1.2816 (80%) and 1.96 (95%). Empirical coverage in the backtest: 80% intervals covered {ff['interval_coverage']['1']['cover80']*100:.0f}%, {ff['interval_coverage']['2']['cover80']*100:.0f}% and {ff['interval_coverage']['3']['cover80']*100:.0f}% of outcomes at h = 1, 2, 3 (n = 15, 14, 13). Beyond h = 3 the √h scaling is an untested assumption.")
h2(d, "5.5  Ensemble selection and its bias")
para(d, "The first run used a four-member ensemble including ETS and recent-drift. Backtests showed those two were weakest at 2–3 year horizons (ETS 9.0% MAPE at h = 3), so the ensemble was changed to drift, ARIMA and log-linear trend. This is a selection step on the same data used to evaluate the result, so reported ensemble accuracy is optimistic. The forecast level is robust to the choice: retained members span 24.0–25.6 TWh in 2035.")
h2(d, "5.6  Elasticity estimation")
bullets(d, [f"Growth form: Δln D_{{it}} = α + β Δln GDP_{{it}} + u. Pooled over nine countries with country-clustered standard errors (CR1 small-sample correction G/(G−1)·(n−1)/(n−k)): β = {el['pooled_all_10']['elasticity']:.3f}, SE = {el['pooled_all_10']['se']:.3f}, n = {el['pooled_all_10']['n']}.",
            f"Kenya only, classical OLS: β = {el['kenya_only_ols']['elasticity']:.3f}, SE = {el['kenya_only_ols']['se']:.3f}, p = {el['kenya_only_ols']['p']:.2f}, R² = {el['kenya_only_ols']['r2']:.3f}, n = {el['kenya_only_ols']['n']}.",
            f"Levels form with country fixed effects (within transformation): ln(D/pop)_{{it}} = α_i + β ln(GDP/pop)_{{it}}: β = {el['fe_loglog_levels']['elasticity']:.3f}, n = {el['fe_loglog_levels']['n']}. Standard errors not reported for this descriptive estimate."])
para(d, "With nine clusters, cluster-robust inference is itself unreliable; the estimates are presented as descriptive.")
h2(d, "5.7  Backtest results")
s = pd.read_csv(T+"backtest_summary.csv"); rows = []
for m in ["Ensemble (mean)", "Drift (RW+drift)", "Log-linear trend", "GDP-driver (conditional)", "ARIMA(1,1,0)+drift", "Recent drift (8y)", "ETS damped trend"]:
    r = [m]
    for h in (1,2,3):
        x = s[(s.model==m)&(s.h==h)].iloc[0]; r += [f"{x.MAPE:.2f}", f"{x.RMSE:.2f}", f"{x.bias_pct:+.1f}"]
    rows.append(r)
table(d, pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=["Model", "MAPE h1", "RMSE h1", "Bias% h1", "MAPE h2", "RMSE h2", "Bias% h2", "MAPE h3", "RMSE h3", "Bias% h3"]), caption="Table 5. Backtest metrics (MAPE and bias in %, RMSE in TWh)", fs=7.5)

# 6
h1(d, "6  Stage 3: supply-security index, stress tests and capacity gap")
h2(d, "6.1  Index construction")
para(d, "For each country-year (2006 onward, so the volatility window is available) compute four raw indicators:")
bullets(d, ["Import dependence  I = max(0, net imports / demand) × 100.", "Concentration  HHI = Σ_j s_j², over nine source shares s_j of generation (hydro, geothermal/other renewables, wind, solar, bioenergy, coal, gas, other fossil, nuclear).",
            "Fuel exposure  F = fossil generation / total generation × 100.", "Hydrology risk  H = hydro share (%) × σ_8, where σ_8 is the rolling standard deviation of annual hydro growth over the last eight years (minimum five). Units: percentage of generation exposed to a one-σ hydrology swing."])
para(d, "Each indicator x is winsorised at the pooled 5th and 95th percentiles, then scored s = 100 × (1 − (x − lo)/(hi − lo)), so higher is more secure. The index is the arithmetic mean of the four scores (SSI) and, as a robustness check, the geometric mean (SSI_geo). Pooled scaling means scores are comparable across countries and years.")
h2(d, "6.2  Robustness to weights")
para(d, f"10,000 weight vectors were drawn from Dirichlet(1,1,1,1) and Kenya's 2024 rank recomputed for each: median {f3['kenya_rank_median']:.0f}, first in {f3['kenya_rank_dist_2024']['1']*100:.0f}% of draws, 5th–95th percentile {f3['kenya_rank_p5_p95'][0]}–{f3['kenya_rank_p5_p95'][1]}. Corner checks (70% on one pillar, 10% on others) gave Kenya ranks 7 (imports), 1 (concentration), 3 (fuel exposure), 1 (hydrology). The corner checks were run interactively while verifying the report text and are reported in the Final Report, Section 5.4.")
h2(d, "6.3  Stress tests")
si = f3["stress_inputs"]
para(d, f"Base: Kenya's 2025 supply stack. Thermal ceiling = {si['thermal_capacity_gw']} GW × 8.76 TWh/GW × CF_th, with CF_th = {si['thermal_cf_assumed']} → {si['thermal_ceiling']:.3f} TWh; 2025 thermal output {si['thermal_2025']:.3f} TWh; headroom {si['thermal_headroom']:.3f} TWh. For a scenario with hydro shock a_h, wind shock a_w, geothermal shock a_g and import multiplier m: lost = −a_h·Hydro − a_w·Wind − a_g·Geo + (1 − m)·Imports; thermal_add = min(lost, headroom); unserved = lost − thermal_add; extra CO2 = thermal_add × 0.658 Mt/TWh (Ember 'Other fossil' intensity, 658 g/kWh).")
table(d, pd.DataFrame([["S1", "Hydro −25%", "Moderate drought; about the 2009-sized shock (−29% vs trend)"], ["S2", "Hydro −42%", "Equals 2000 deviation from 5-year centred mean"], ["S3", "Imports × 0", "Total interconnector loss"], ["S4", "Hydro −25%, imports × 0.5", "Moderate compound"],
                       ["S5", "Hydro −42%, imports × 0", "Severe compound"], ["S6", "S5 + wind −20% + geothermal −10%", "Extreme compound; illustrative"]], columns=["ID", "Shocks", "Rationale"]), caption="Table 6. Deterministic scenarios", fs=8.5, widths=[1.2, 5.6, 9.4], align_right_from=9)
para(d, "Monte Carlo: for each of 20,000 draws, sample one historical year and take hydro and geothermal deviations from their centred five-year means (joint draw, preserving co-movement); apply them to the 2025 hydro and geothermal output; compute lost energy (negative changes only), add the full import volume in the 'imports interrupted' state, and subtract thermal headroom to obtain unserved energy. Probabilities are conditional on the import state; no probability is assigned to an import outage.")
h2(d, "6.4  Capacity-gap scenarios")
para(d, "Demand paths: forecast lower 80% bound, ensemble, upper 80% bound. Gap_t = D_t − D_2025 (imports and thermal held at 2025 volumes). New capacity for technology j with energy share w_j and capacity factor CF_j: GW = Σ_j Gap·w_j / (8.76·CF_j). CF: geothermal 0.85, wind 0.45, solar 0.25. Mixes (geothermal/wind/solar energy shares): 50/30/20, 34/33/33, 20/30/50. Variant B adds the energy needed to bring imports and thermal output down to 5% of demand each.")
h2(d, "6.5  Historical build pace")
para(d, f"Generation grew {f3['hist_gen_addition_twh_per_yr_2015_2025']:.3f} TWh/year over 2015–2025 and {f3['hist_gen_addition_twh_per_yr_2020_2025']:.3f} TWh/year over 2020–2025. Required base-case pace to 2035 is {f3['req_pace_base_2035']:.3f} TWh/year, a multiple of {f3['pace_multiple_base_2035']:.2f} of the ten-year pace.")

# 7
h1(d, "7  Stage 4: visualisation")
para(d, "Eleven figures were produced with matplotlib at 200 dpi on a light surface. The categorical colours follow a documented eight-hue default order (blue, orange, aqua, amber, magenta, green, violet, red) applied in fixed order; where a figure highlights Kenya against peers, Kenya is blue and peers are neutral grey. Text uses neutral ink, never series colours. The colour-vision validator that accompanies the palette guidance was not re-run on these specific figures; adjacent hues were used as documented, and every multi-series chart carries a legend or direct labels. Layout problems (legend overlap, label collisions, clipped legend) were found by viewing rendered images and corrected; the stress-test figure needed two passes. The fossil category in the supply-mix chart uses neutral grey and not a palette hue, to avoid implying that fossil is 'green'.")

# 8
h1(d, "8  Stage 5: report generation")
para(d, "Reports are written with python-docx using a shared helper module (styles, headed tables, captions, callouts, code blocks) and converted to PDF with LibreOffice. Quantities in the text are f-string substitutions from the outputs, which is why the Final Report can be regenerated after a data refresh with no manual edits to numbers. After generation, pages were rendered to images and inspected; one orphaned page was fixed by merging two bullets and removing a table row.")

h2(d, "8.1  Interactive dashboard (Stage 6)")
para(d, "src/07_build_dashboard.py reads the processed tables, packages the series as JSON and injects them into src/dashboard_template.html, producing index.html at the repository root (the GitHub Pages entry point): one self-contained file (about 80 KB, no build step, no server) that runs offline apart from the web fonts. Charts are drawn as SVG by hand-written JavaScript; there is no charting library. Four views carry live controls that recompute in the browser: forecast scenario and model comparison; pillar weights that re-rank the ten countries; a stress simulator (hydro, wind, geothermal, import and thermal-availability sliders) with a 20,000-draw seeded Monte Carlo on the observed hydrology deviations; and a capacity calculator (demand path, year, technology mix, capacity factors). The in-browser formulas are the same as in Stage 3, and the preset buttons were checked against the report: Kenya's rank under the four 70%-weight presets is 7, 3, 1, 1, the import-loss scenario returns 1.52 TWh replaced by thermal and 1.00 Mt CO2, and the phase-down option gives 2.3 GW for the base 2035 case. The browser Monte Carlo uses a different random generator from the Python one, so its shortfall probability is approximately, not exactly, the 27% in the report. The page was tested in Chromium at 1280 px and 400 px widths, in dark and light themes, with no console errors and no horizontal overflow.")
